Dan says (on the subject of dropping one's stuff before dying): 
>I often do this intentionlly.  Ill reroll fo wiz until I get a WoP,
>then Ill run down to level 5 and die on purpose (leaving my stuff by
>the stairs and running to the other side of the maze).  Then Ill play
>a Valk or Samuri and go down to the bones level look for the stuff.
>If I dont get it, I quit and try again.

>Other bones-games I play, is to reroll a Valk until he gets an oil lamp,
>mad dash for the gnome town, see if there are any magic lamps, committ
>harey carey on the level below if there are, in an attempt to get multiple
>wish lamps for my next character.  I figure between the lighting store and
>the tools shop, one can get a magic lamp 1/3rd-1/4th of the town levels.

Jeez, isn't this going a little overboard?  I've got nowhere near as 
much patience as you do.  If you're going to do this kind of stuff why
not just play in explore mode and get a free wand of wishing?  It's 
quicker and more honest.  I wonder if you could make it to the castle 
level quicker than you can go through all this rerolling and muliple
suicides.  You only need so many wishes to get all the good stuff.

Of course, I admire your cleverness.  I never would have thought of
doing anything quite this devious.  My only concession to future bones
levels is to avoid annoying shopkeepers out of fear for less powerful
namesakes.  And to occasionally yell "Please please please bones bones
bones" like a moron when I die with good stuff.  I don't see them as the
danger that some of you seem to; I see bones as great sources of great,
albeit cursed, stuff.  But I don't intentionally kill off perfectly
good characters to try to get them.  Especially a character lucky 
enough to already have a wish by the Gnomish Mines.    

[Not a flame; I'm not a true purist, as I polypile sometimes.]


